@page "/"
@inject SiteInfo Site

<PageHead Title="" Description="@Site.Description" />

<div class="hero">
    <h1>Write pages as Razor components. Ship static HTML.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
        Kiji is a static site generator framework for .NET. Pages are Razor components
        rendered through Blazor's <code>HtmlRenderer</code>, assembled with a
        minimal-API style builder you already know from ASP.NET Core.
    </p>
    <p class="cta">
        <a class="button" href="@Site.Path("docs/getting-started/")">Get started</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/zzzkan/kiji">Source on GitHub</a>
    </p>
</div>

<section class="pitch">
    <article>
        <h2>One package</h2>
        <p>
            Markdown with YAML front matter, responsive WebP image optimization, RSS
            feeds, sitemaps, and a live-reloading dev server are all in the
            <code>Kiji</code> package. No hunting for extensions to combine.
        </p>
    </article>

    <article>
        <h2>Incremental by default</h2>
        <p>
            Every build records what each page read — content files, the content set,
            options, your assemblies — and skips pages whose inputs are unchanged.
            Editing one post re-renders that post, the pages that list it, and the
            artifacts. Anything ambiguous falls back to a full rebuild: stale output is
            never acceptable.
        </p>
    </article>

    <article>
        <h2>A dev loop that ignores your site's size</h2>
        <p>
            The dev server pre-generates nothing. It renders the page you
            requested, through the same code path the build uses, so what you see is
            what gets deployed — and a site with five thousand posts reloads as fast as
            one with five.
        </p>
    </article>

    <article>
        <h2>Correctness first</h2>
        <p>
            Duplicate routes, output path collisions, missing route values, and paths
            that escape the output directory all fail while planning, before a single
            file is written.
        </p>
    </article>
</section>

<section class="quickstart">
    <h2>Getting started</h2>
    <pre><code>dotnet add package Kiji</code></pre>
    <p>
        Then declare your site. <a href="@Site.Path("docs/getting-started/")">The full
        walkthrough</a> covers the project layout and the CLI.
    </p>
</section>
